# =====================================================================
#  PART TWO: THE INGENIOUS KNIGHT - English text
#  Locations, objects and long messages (same names as parte2_mundo.paw)
# =====================================================================

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the house
/LOC alcoba2
Your bedchamber. You have spent nearly a month in this bed, so dried
up and withered that you look like nothing so much as a mummy. The
door leads south, to the hall.

/LOC sala2
The hall of your house. On the rack, as always, there is room for a
lance. To the north is your bedchamber and to the south the way out to
the yard. Where the library used to be, the whitewashed wall is still
there.

/LOC corral3
The yard of your house. Rocinante, who has put on a little weight this
month, is feeling it in his soul. The back door, unnailed long ago,
opens onto the fields to the east. The hall lies to the north.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- El Toboso
/LOC camino_toboso
The road to El Toboso. The two of you ride alone, unseen: you talking
of Dulcinea and Sancho trembling, because he has never seen her in his
life and he knows it. To the east a huddle of houses can be seen.

/LOC toboso
The great city of El Toboso, at midnight. Nothing is heard in the
whole place but the barking of dogs, which deafens the ears: now and
then an ass brays, pigs grunt and cats mew. To the north rises a great
building; to the east a narrow lane runs off; to the south, outside the
town, there is an oak grove. The road goes back to the west.

/LOC iglesia
A great tower casting its shadow in the middle of the night. You were
looking for a palace. This is the parish church. The square lies to
the south.

/LOC callejuela
A narrow, dark blind alley. There are no palaces or alcazars here,
only the low houses of farm folk. The square lies to the west.

/LOC encinar_toboso
An oak grove half a league from El Toboso. It is daytime. From here
you can see the town, to the north, and the road that goes on to the
east.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- on the road
/LOC camino_carreta
A broad road between vineyards. The road goes back to the west, to the
oak grove, and on to the east.

/LOC bosque_espejos
A dark night under some tall, shadowy trees. You have dismounted to
sleep. Very close, at the foot of a tree, someone is sighing for love
and tuning a lute. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC camino_leones
A highroad, within sight of the house of the Knight of the Green
Coat, Don Diego de Miranda, who is riding with you. Along it comes a
cart flying the king's flags. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC prado_bodas
A meadow at the edge of a village, turned into a kitchen. There is a
whole bullock spitted on an elm trunk, six pots as big as wine jars and
mountains of white bread. It all belongs to Camacho the rich, who is
getting married today. To the north, under a bower, the wedding is
being held. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC enramada
A bower of trees decorated as for a feast. Here are the bride and
groom, the priest and half the village. The meadow with the pots lies
to the south.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the cave
/LOC boca_cueva
The mouth of the cave of Montesinos, wide and spacious, but full of
boxthorn, wild fig, brambles and briars, so thick and tangled that
they completely blind and cover it. The road goes back to the west, to
the wedding, and on to the east.

/LOC cueva1
It is as dark as a wolf's mouth. You hang from the rope, which Sancho
and the cousin are paying out little by little. You can keep going
down.

/LOC cueva2
A black, damp passage. The echo gives you back your own voice, calling
for Dulcinea. There are galleries in every direction.

/LOC cueva3
Another passage, as black as the last. Or it is the same one. There
are galleries in every direction.

/LOC cueva4
A hollow in one side of the cave, big enough to hold a cart with its
mules. A little light comes in through some cracks. There are
galleries in every direction.

/LOC palacio_cristal
A meadow, the loveliest that nature could create. In the middle of it
rises a royal and splendid palace whose walls seem to be built of
clear crystal. Out of its doors comes a venerable old man in a hooded
cloak of purple baize: Montesinos.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- to the Ebro
/LOC venta_retablo
An inn, which this time, by some miracle, you take for an inn. In the
yard Master Pedro, a puppeteer with a patch of green taffeta covering
half his face, has set up his show. On his shoulder he carries a
tailless ape. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC orilla_ebro
The banks of the Ebro, with gentle, plentiful waters. By the bank,
tied to the trunk of a tree, there is a small boat without oars or any
other tackle. The road goes on to the east.

/LOC barco_rio
You are in the enchanted boat, drifting slowly downstream, without oars
or sails. You say you have sailed seven or eight hundred leagues.
Sancho says he can still see Rocinante and the dapple on the bank.
Ahead, some great watermills stand in the middle of the river.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the dukes
/LOC prado_caza
A green meadow at the edge of a wood, at sunset. There is a hunting
party: a gallant lady on a snow-white palfrey, with a hawk on her left
hand. A great lady. The road goes on to the east, to a castle.

/LOC castillo_patio
The courtyard of the dukes' castle. This is the first time you know
and believe yourself to be a real knight errant, and not an imaginary
one. To the north is the great hall and to the east the garden. The
road leaves to the south.

/LOC castillo_salon
A great hall hung with very rich cloth of gold and brocade. The table
is laid. The dukes are dining with a grave ecclesiastic, one of those
who rule the households of princes. The courtyard lies to the south.

/LOC jardin
The castle garden, as evening falls. Fountains, mazes of myrtle and a
few servants who hide their laughter very badly. The courtyard lies to
the west.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- Barataria
/LOC barataria
The island of Barataria, which is a town of about a thousand souls,
one of the best the duke owns. As for being an island, it isn't much
of one: it has no sea. To the north is the courthouse, to the east the
governor's palace and to the west the streets of the town.

/LOC juzgado
The courtroom. They seat you in the judge's chair. On the wall there
are letters saying that on such a day the lord Don Sancho Panza took
possession of this island. The way out is to the south.

/LOC comedor
A room in the palace, with a royal and spotless table. Beside you,
standing, is a personage with a whalebone wand in his hand: Doctor
Pedro Recio de Agüero, native of Tirteafuera. To the north is your
bedchamber and to the west the square.

/LOC alcoba_gob
The governor's bedchamber: a soft bed, so soft you don't trust it. The
way out is to the south.

/LOC ronda
The streets of the island, at night. You are making the rounds with
the steward, the secretary, the head carver and a chronicler who writes
everything down. The square lies to the east.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the pit and Barcelona
/LOC camino_sima
A road half a league from the dukes' castle. On one side a deep, dark
pit opens up. The castle lies to the north and the road goes on to the
east, towards Barcelona.

/LOC ciudad
Barcelona: the treasure house of courtesy, the shelter of strangers,
the refuge of the poor, the home of the valiant. You are staying with
Don Antonio Moreno, a rich and discreet gentleman, in his house. To the
south is the way down to the beach.

/LOC playa
The beach of Barcelona. You see the sea, which you had never seen
before: it seems to you vast and wide, far bigger than the lakes of
Ruidera. In the harbour there are galleys with pennants and streamers.
The city lies to the north.

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the way home
/LOC camino_vuelta
The road back to La Mancha. You ride unarmed and as a traveller;
Sancho goes on foot, because the dapple carries your armour, like a
trophy in reverse. The road goes on to the west.

/LOC vuelta_toboso
A crossroads within sight of El Toboso. From here you can see the tower
of the church you came upon that night. To the north, among olive
trees, runs the road to El Toboso; to the west, the road to your
village.

/LOC dos_muchachas
You find yourself before two girls: one richly dressed, like a
princess; the other is a peasant. Both say they are Dulcinea del
Toboso.

/LOC aldea2
The edge of your village. Some boys are quarrelling on the threshing
floors, and a hare runs past, fleeing from some greyhounds, and hides
under the dapple. Your house is to the north.

# =====================================================================
#  OBJECTS
# =====================================================================
/OBJ reales
the four reals
/OBJ soga
the hundred-fathom rope
/OBJ joya
the jewel
/OBJ panuelo
a holland handkerchief
/OBJ baculo
the old man's staff

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: THE HOUSE AND THE THIRD SALLY
# =====================================================================
/MSG segunda_parte
{i6}{b1}        PART TWO{b0}{i7}{n}{n}{i5}  The ingenious knight{n}  Don Quixote of La Mancha{i7}

/MSG visita_cura
Nearly a month goes by without the curate and the barber visiting you,
so as not to bring past events back to your memory. At last they come.
You talk with them of statecraft and modes of government with such
good sense that they believe you entirely cured.

Until the curate, to test you, brings up the news that the Turk is
coming down with a mighty fleet. {i4}"What his majesty should do is
order all the knights errant who roam about Spain to assemble at court
on a fixed day, for even if no more than half a dozen came, there
might be one among them able by himself to destroy the whole power of
the Turk."{i7}

The barber sighs. The curate crosses himself. And they leave.

/MSG sancho_noticia
Just then voices are heard in the entrance: the housekeeper and the
niece won't let Sancho in, though he has come to see his master. Sancho
comes in anyway.

{i5}"Last night Bartolomé Carrasco's son came back from his studies at
Salamanca, a bachelor now, and he told me your worship's history is
already in a book, called {i6}The Ingenious Gentleman Don Quixote of La
Mancha{i5}. And that they mention me in it by my own name of Sancho
Panza, and the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, and other things that
happened to us when we were alone, so that I crossed myself in wonder
at how the historian could know them."{i7}

And off he runs to fetch the bachelor.

/MSG sanson_habla
The bachelor Samson Carrasco is not very big in body, but a very great
wag, sallow of complexion, but of very good understanding. He goes down
on his knees before you: {i6}"Give me your hands, Señor Don Quixote of
La Mancha."{i7}

He tells you that more than twelve thousand copies of your history are
in print: in Portugal, in Barcelona, in Valencia, and even in Antwerp.
That children turn its leaves, young people read it, grown men
understand it and old folk praise it. And that some find fault with
it: that the author forgot to tell who stole Sancho's dapple, and that
later, without saying how, Sancho turns up riding the very same ass.

{i5}"That was Ginés de Pasamonte!"{i7} shouts Sancho. {i6}"And they say
there will be a second part,"{i7} adds the bachelor. {i6}"My advice is
that your worship should go to Saragossa, to the jousts in honour of
Saint George."{i7} As he leaves, without your seeing it, he winks at
the curate.

/MSG pide_salario
Sancho plants himself in front of you and scratches the back of his
neck: {i5}"Teresa says I should tie my finger well with your worship,
for a bird in the hand is worth two in the bush. So I'd like your
worship to fix me a known wage, of what you'll give me each month for
as long as I serve you."{i7}

/MSG sin_salario
{i4}"Look, Sancho: I have read all or most of the histories of knights
errant, and I do not remember ever reading that any knight errant
fixed a known wage for his squire. If thou wilt not come with me on
trust, God be with thee, for I shall not want for squires."{i7}

Sancho is taken aback, and more so when the bachelor, who is passing
by, offers himself as your squire. His eyes cloud over: {i5}"Here I
am, sir, to serve you faithfully and loyally, as well as and better
than any squire who ever served a knight errant in times past or
present."{i7} And he leaves a new pair of saddlebags on the floor for
you.

/MSG tercera_salida
At nightfall, without anyone knowing but the bachelor, who rides half
a league with you, you set off on the road to El Toboso. You on your
good Rocinante and Sancho on his old dapple, the saddlebags well
furnished with things to eat. Samson embraces you both, wishes you a
good journey and goes back to the village. He smiles more than he
should.

Rocinante neighs and the dapple brays, which you both take for a good
omen.

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: EL TOBOSO
# =====================================================================
/MSG toboso_llega
{i4}"Sancho, my son, lead on to Dulcinea's palace, for perhaps we may
find her awake."{i7}

{i5}"What palace am I to lead to, body of the sun, when the one I saw
her highness in was nothing but a very little house?"{i7}

Sancho has never seen Dulcinea in his life, and neither have you,
though you often forget the latter.

/MSG iglesia
You head for the great mass casting its shadow and come upon a tall
tower. {i4}"It is the church we have come upon, Sancho."{i7}

{i5}"So I see, and God grant we don't come upon our graves, for it's
no good sign to wander in churchyards at such hours."{i7}

/MSG labrador_toboso
Along the lane comes a farmer with two mules, going out to plough very
early, singing the ballad of {i6}"Ill went it with you, Frenchmen, at
Roncesvalles"{i7}.

/MSG labrador_responde
{i6}"Sir, I'm a stranger here and it's only a few days since I came to
this town, to work the land for a rich farmer. There's no princess
here at all. Plenty of fine ladies, yes, for each one can be a
princess in her own house."{i7}

And off he goes, urging on his mules, without waiting for more
questions.

/MSG sancho_busca
{i5}"Sir, the day is coming on, and it won't be wise to let the sun
find us in the street. Better we go out of the city, and your worship
hide in some wood near here, and I'll come back by day and leave no
corner in this whole place where I don't look for my lady's house,
alcazar or palace."{i7}

Sancho goes into the town. As he leaves, you hear him talking to
himself: {i5}"This master of mine, by a thousand signs I've seen, is a
madman fit to be tied... And being so, it won't be very hard to make
him believe that a country girl, the first I come across here, is the
lady Dulcinea."{i7}

/MSG tres_labradoras
After a good while Sancho comes running back: {i5}"Good news, sir!
There's nothing to be done but for your worship to spur Rocinante and
come out into the open to see the lady Dulcinea del Toboso, who is
coming to see your worship with two of her damsels."{i7}

Along the road come three country girls on three young asses. All you
see is three country girls on three donkeys. Sancho sees three
princesses on three piebald hackneys.

/MSG encantada
You go down on your knees beside Sancho before one of them, who is
round-faced, snub-nosed and rather chubby. {i6}"Get out of the way, bad
luck to you, and let us pass, for we are in a hurry!"{i7} says the
country girl.

She prods her donkey, which throws her to the ground. Without waiting
for anyone, she takes a little run, puts her hands on the donkey's
haunches and lands, lighter than a hawk, on the pack saddle. And off
the three of them go at full speed. A smell of raw garlic reaches you
that turns your stomach and poisons your soul.

{i4}"Seest thou, Sancho, how the enchanters hate me? See how far their
malice reaches: they have changed my Dulcinea into a country
girl."{i7} Sancho covers his mouth so as not to laugh.

{i6}Dulcinea is enchanted. And the peasant girl is Dulcinea.{i7}

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: THE PARLIAMENT OF DEATH, THE MIRRORS AND THE LIONS
# =====================================================================
/MSG carreta
Across the road comes a cart loaded with the most varied and strange
figures. The one driving the mules is an ugly demon. The cart is open
to the sky: the first figure is Death himself, with a human face;
beside him, an angel with large painted wings; to one side, an emperor
with a crown, apparently of gold, and at Death's feet the god Cupid,
without his blindfold, but with his bow and quiver.

/MSG cortes
{i6}"Sir, we are players of the company of Angulo el Malo. This morning
we performed the play {i5}The Parliament of Death{i6}, and we are to
perform it this afternoon in that village you can see from here. And
as it is so near, we came dressed in the very costumes we act in."{i7}

{i4}"Go with God, good people, and hold your festival."{i7}

Just then one of the company comes up dressed as a jester, with a lot
of bells and three blown-up cow bladders on the end of a stick. He
shakes the bladders next to Rocinante, who takes fright and throws you
to the ground. The demon from the cart jumps on the dapple and gallops
off towards the village. A little later the dapple throws him and comes
back by himself to his master.

/MSG piedras
The people of the cart, seeing you coming, jump down and line up, with
stones in their hands: Death, the emperor, the demon driver, the
angel, the queen and the god Cupid.

{i5}"Look, sir, among all those people there, though they look like
kings, princes and emperors, there isn't a single knight errant. And
your worship mustn't lay hand to sword against anyone who hasn't been
dubbed a knight."{i7}

/MSG muerte_piedras
You charge again. The players are not knights, but they aim as if they
were. A shower of pebbles leaves you lying under the cart, and Death,
very much in character, closes your eyes. {i2}You are dead, and this
time it isn't theatre.{i7}

/MSG espejos_llega
At the foot of a tree another knight is singing a sonnet to his lady
to the sound of a lute: {i6}"Grant me, lady, a fixed term to
follow..."{i7} He wears a surcoat that seems to be of the finest gold,
sown with many little moons of shining mirrors. There is another
knight here beside his mount.

/MSG espejos_hola
{i6}"I am the Knight of the Mirrors."{i7}

{i6}"Tell me, your worship: is not Casildea de Vandalia the most
beautiful maiden? Answer me."{i7}

/MSG espejos_no
{i6}"What, no? Prepare yourself for combat!!"{i7}

Down come the visors. Your squire and the squire of the Mirrors, who
has a frightful nose, withdraw to a prudent distance.

/MSG espejos_si
{i6}"I knew well you would not fight, your worship is a coward."{i7}

You repent of your cowardice and decide to leave the order of
chivalry. You go back home and stay there weeping over your decision.

/MSG espejos_gana
You strike the knight in the chest with your lance and unhorse him.

{i6}"I yield. I know your glorious deeds: the most beautiful is without
doubt Dulcinea del Toboso."{i7}

You take off his helmet to see whether he is alive and you find... the
very face, the very figure, the very look, the very features, the very
likeness of the bachelor Samson Carrasco. His squire takes off his
nose, which was made of pasteboard, and turns out to be Tomé Cecial,
Sancho's neighbour and crony.

{i4}"Enchanters! They have taken on the bachelor's form to rob me of
the glory of my victory."{i7} Sancho doesn't know what to think. You
do.

/MSG espejos_huye
You try to escape and the Knight of the Mirrors runs you through the
back with his lance. {i2}You are dead.{i7}

/MSG espejos_vence
The Knight of the Mirrors defeats you. You go back to your village and
leave the order of chivalry.

/MSG leones_llega
Along the road comes a cart crowned with royal flags. Sancho, who had
bought some curds from some shepherds, doesn't know where to put them
when you call him in a hurry, and puts them in your helmet, which you
had taken off for a moment because of the heat.

/MSG requesones
You clap the helmet on and the whey begins to run all over your face
and beard. {i4}"What can this be, Sancho, for it seems as if my skull
were softening, or my brains melting, or that I am sweating from head
to foot?"{i7}

/MSG leonero
The carter, who is the lion keeper, explains: {i6}"In the cart there are
two fierce lions in cages, which the general of Oran is sending to
court as a present for his majesty. Hungry, for they haven't eaten
today."{i7}

{i4}"Lion whelps to me? To me lion whelps, and at such a time? Then, by
God, those gentlemen who send them here shall see if I am a man to be
frightened by lions. Open those cages."{i7}

/MSG jaula_abierta
The lion keeper, trembling, flings the first cage wide open. Don Diego
de Miranda spurs his mare, Sancho his dapple and the carter his mules,
and they take refuge far away.

The lion turns round in the cage, stretches out a paw, stretches
himself, opens his mouth and yawns very slowly. Then he puts his head
out and looks all around with eyes like live coals. There is a lion
here with a hungry look on his face.

/MSG cantar_leon
{i2}"La donna è mobile qual piuma al vento..."{i7}

The lion begins to fall asleep and goes off to look for a more
comfortable place to have a nap. You can see a jewel on the ground.

/MSG caballero_leones
The lion keeper shuts the cage. They all come back and you give the
keeper two gold crowns to tell his majesty what he has seen. {i4}"If
his majesty should ask who performed it, thou shalt say the Knight of
the Lions, for from this time forward I mean that this name shall
replace, change, transform and alter the one I have borne until now,
of the Knight of the Rueful Countenance."{i7}

/MSG leon_come
The lion eats you and puts an end to your glorious career as a knight.

/MSG leon_mata
You attack the lion, but his strength is greater than yours and he
kills you. {i2}You are dead and devoured.{i7}

/MSG joya_coger
A gold jewel with a stone that shines like a piece of the sun. You keep
it: it is worthy of Dulcinea. Of the old one, the one who didn't smell
of garlic.

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: CAMACHO'S WEDDING AND THE CAVE OF MONTESINOS
# =====================================================================
/MSG bodas_prado
Sancho stares at the pots like a child at a shop window. From the elms
hang hares already skinned and hens already plucked. There are more
than fifty cooks, all clean, all busy and all cheerful.

/MSG espuma
Sancho goes up to one of the cooks and begs him, in courteous and
hungry terms, to let him dip a crust of bread in one of those pots.
{i6}"Brother, this is not one of those days over which hunger has any
jurisdiction. Skim off a hen or two, and much good may they do
you."{i7}

The cook takes a ladle, plunges it into one of the jars and brings out
three hens and two geese. Sancho sits down to eat with more appetite
than in the whole of Part One.

/MSG boda
The bride and groom arrive. Quiteria is radiant, with her corals and
her velvet. Camacho, rich and pleased. Then in comes a man dressed in
black, with a wreath of mournful cypress and a great staff in his
hands: it is Basilio, the poor shepherd who has loved Quiteria since
they were children.

{i6}"Thou knowest well, ungrateful Quiteria, that thou didst promise to
be my wife. Well, my death will clear the way to thy happiness."{i7} He
drives the staff into the ground; hidden inside it was a short sword,
and he throws himself upon it. He falls bathed in blood.

With what voice he has left, he asks that Quiteria give him her hand
as his wife before he dies. Camacho doesn't know what to say.

/MSG camacho_consiente
{i4}"It is just and reasonable, Señor Camacho, what this youth asks.
And your worship will be as much honoured in receiving Quiteria as the
widow of so valiant a husband as if you received her from her father's
side."{i7}

Camacho, thinking the wounded man will die before you can say two
credos, consents. The priest marries them. And no sooner has Quiteria
said yes than Basilio springs to his feet and pulls the sword out of
his body, which went into an iron tube full of prepared blood. {i6}"No
miracle, miracle, but trick, trick!"{i7}

Camacho's friends lay hand to their swords.

/MSG bodas_paz
You place yourself between them, on horseback, with your lance on your
arm: {i4}"Hold, sirs, hold, for it is not right to take vengeance for
the wrongs that love does to us. Quiteria was Basilio's, and Basilio
Quiteria's, by the just and favourable disposition of heaven. And
those whom God has joined, let no man put asunder."{i7}

Camacho, seeing that Quiteria doesn't love him, decides his pots are
worth more to him. The wedding goes on as if nothing had happened.
Basilio thanks you with a cousin of his, a famous student and great
humanist, who offers to guide you to the cave of Montesinos. And he
gives you a rope of a hundred fathoms, for going down a cave you need
one badly.

/MSG boca_llega
{i6}"This is the cave of Montesinos,"{i7} says the cousin, who is
writing a book of the Spanish Ovid and another of the inventors of
things. {i6}"They tell great marvels about it. And it's very deep."{i7}

/MSG cuervos
You draw your sword and begin to cut down and hack away the brambles.
At the noise, out of the mouth of the cave fly countless enormous crows
and jackdaws, so thick and in such haste that they knock you to the
ground. Were you as superstitious as you are Catholic, you would take
it for an evil omen.

/MSG soga_atada
Sancho and the cousin tie the rope round your waist, good and tight.
{i5}"God guide you, and the Rock of France, together with the Trinity
of Gaeta, flower, cream and skimmings of knights errant!"{i7}

/MSG bajar_cueva
You commend yourself to Dulcinea and let yourself down into the
depths of the dreadful cavern. Sancho and the cousin pay out the rope,
and you call out for more and more. In the end you stop calling.

/MSG sueno_cueva
You lie down in the hollow, tired of so much darkness, and a very deep
sleep comes over you. When you wake, without knowing how or why, you
find yourself in the middle of the most beautiful, pleasant and
delightful meadow that nature could create.

/MSG montesinos
{i6}"Long ages have we who are enchanted in these solitudes been
waiting to see thee, valiant knight Don Quixote of La Mancha."{i7}

Montesinos leads you before a marble tomb on which lies the knight
Durandarte, flower and mirror of lovelorn knights. Above, in a line,
passes a procession of damsels, and behind them the lady Belerma, with
dark rings under her eyes and Durandarte's heart in her hands, in a
cloth of holland, which he himself cut out with a dagger. And we
salted it, so that it would reach her smelling sweet.

{i6}"Patience, and shuffle the cards,"{i7} sighs Durandarte from the
tomb, quite rightly.

/MSG tres_labradoras_cueva
Across the meadow, leaping and skipping like goats, come three country
girls. One of them is the peerless Dulcinea del Toboso, enchanted, the
same one you saw outside El Toboso. You call to her and she turns her
back on you.

One of her companions comes up: {i6}"My lady Dulcinea kisses your
worship's hands and begs you to let her know how you are. And, being in
great need, she begs your worship to lend her half a dozen reals on
this new cotton petticoat I have here."{i7}

/MSG cuatro_reales
{i4}"Tell your lady, my friend, that her troubles grieve me to the soul,
and that I would I were a Fugger to remedy them. And that I have no
more than four reals."{i7}

You give them to her. Instead of making you a curtsey, she cuts a
caper that lifts her two yards into the air. The petticoat she leaves
with you.

/MSG sacan_cueva
Up above they pull on the rope. They bring you out of the cave with
your eyes closed, as if asleep. {i4}"God forgive you, friends, for you
have taken me away from the sweetest and most delightful existence and
spectacle that ever human being enjoyed or beheld."{i7}

You tell them you have been down there three days. Sancho swears it
was little more than an hour. And when you tell them about the four
reals, Sancho slaps his forehead, because it was he who enchanted
Dulcinea, and now it turns out the enchanters are competing with him.

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: MASTER PEDRO'S PUPPET SHOW AND THE ENCHANTED BOAT
# =====================================================================
/MSG maese_pedro
{i6}"This ape talks, but his master understands him: he divines the
past and the present, though of the future he knows nothing."{i7} The
moment the ape sees you, he jumps onto Master Pedro's shoulder and
whispers something in his ear.

{i6}"O glorious reviver of knight-errantry, now so forgotten! O never
duly praised knight Don Quixote of La Mancha!"{i7} cries Master Pedro,
falling on his knees.

/MSG mono_responde
You ask the ape whether what you saw in the cave of Montesinos was
real or a dream. The ape jumps, chatters and talks into his master's
ear.

{i6}"The ape says that some of the things your worship saw or went
through in that cave are false, and some likely. And that he knows no
more, because his powers have run out."{i7}

/MSG retablo_funcion
You sit in the front row. A boy announces the show: {i6}"This true
story here presented to your worships tells how Señor Don Gaiferos
set free his wife Melisendra, who was a captive in Spain, in the hands
of the Moors, in the city of Sansueña."{i7}

Melisendra lets herself down from the tower onto the crupper of
Gaiferos' horse, and off the two of them go on the road to Paris. But
King Marsilio has the alarm sounded, and with such a din of bells and
kettledrums that the city seems to be falling down, all the Moorish
host rides out in pursuit. {i4}The Moors are about to catch
them.{i7}

/MSG retablo_destrozo
{i4}"Never, while I live, will I permit foul play to be practised in my
presence on so famous a knight and so daring a lover as Don Gaiferos!
Halt, ill-born rabble!"{i7}

You draw your sword and with one bound you are beside the show. With
swift and unheard-of fury you begin to rain slashes on the puppet
Moors: you knock some down, behead others, maim this one, demolish
that one. And among many others you deal such a downstroke that, had
Master Pedro not ducked, you would have cut off his head.

{i6}"Stop, your worship, Señor Don Quixote, and mind that those you
are knocking down are not real Moors, but little figures made of
paste!"{i7}

/MSG retablo_paga
{i4}"Now I truly believe that these enchanters who persecute me put
figures before my eyes as they really are, and then change them into
whatever they please."{i7}

Master Pedro values the damage, figure by figure: King Marsilio,
beheaded, four and a half reals; Charlemagne, with his crown split, five
reals and a quarter... Sancho pays forty reals and three-quarters in
all, and two reals more for the trouble of catching the ape, which has
fled to the roof. Before dawn, Master Pedro disappears. (He was Ginés
de Pasamonte, who covered his eye with a patch so you wouldn't
recognise him.)

/MSG barco
{i4}"Thou must know, Sancho, that this boat here, plainly and beyond
all possibility of doubt, is calling and inviting me to embark in it
and go in it to give aid to some knight or other person of rank in
distress, who must be in some great trouble."{i7}

/MSG aceñas
{i4}"Seest thou? There, O friend, stands revealed the city, castle or
fortress where some oppressed knight or some queen, infanta or princess
in distress must be."{i7} {i5}"What the devil city, fortress or castle
does your worship mean? Can't you see that those are watermills in the
river, where they grind the wheat?"{i7}

The millers, covered in flour, come out with long poles: {i6}"Devils of
men! Where are you going? Have you gone mad? Do you want to drown and
be smashed to pieces in these wheels?"{i7}

You stand up in the boat and threaten the millers with your sword. The
current carries the boat into the race of the wheels; the millers
manage to stop it with their poles, but not without tipping you and
Sancho into the water. They fish you out like two wet cats. The boat
is smashed to pieces.

The fishermen who own the boat arrive, and Sancho pays them fifty reals
very much against his will. {i4}"God help us, for this whole world is
nothing but schemes and contrivances, each against the other. I can do
no more."{i7}

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: THE DUKES, THE DOLORIDA AND CLAVILEÑO
# =====================================================================
/MSG embajada
Sancho gets off the dapple, kneels before the huntress and says:
{i5}"Fair lady, that knight you see over there, called the Knight of
the Lions, is my master, and I am a squire of his, whom they call at
home Sancho Panza."{i7}

The lady turns out to be a duchess, and what's more, she has read Part
One of your history. {i6}"Let him come, and welcome, to make use of us,
for we have a castle near here."{i7}

As you dismount, with nobody to hold your stirrup, your foot gets
caught and you come down full length on the ground. The dukes hide
their laughter as best they can.

/MSG castillo_llega
Before you go in, two lackeys throw over your shoulders a great mantle
of the finest scarlet, and damsels and duennas sprinkle you with flasks
of scented water. {i6}"Welcome to the flower and cream of knights
errant!"{i7} Some grooms take Rocinante to the stables, where they treat
him better than ever in his life.

/MSG eclesiastico_rine
The ecclesiastic, who has heard of your follies, rises from the table:
{i6}"And you, numskull, who has stuck it into your brain that you are a
knight errant, that you vanquish giants and capture malefactors? Go
your way, and go home, and bring up your children if you have any, and
look after your property, and stop wandering about the world."{i7}

/MSG respuesta_eclesiastico
You rise, trembling from head to foot like a man full of quicksilver:
{i4}"Some go by the broad road of proud ambition; others by that of
mean and servile flattery; others by that of deceitful hypocrisy, and
some by that of true religion. But I, led by my star, follow the narrow
path of knight-errantry. A knight I am, and a knight I will die, if it
be the will of the Most High."{i7}

{i5}"Well said, by God! Say no more, your worship, sir and master
mine,"{i7} says Sancho. The ecclesiastic gets up and leaves, saying the
master is as mad as the squire.

/MSG dolorida
A doleful fife sounds. Into the garden comes a man with a long white
beard, Trifaldin of the White Beard, who announces the Countess
Trifaldi, otherwise called the Distressed Duenna. Behind him come twelve
duennas in mourning, and the countess, with a train in three points.

The duennas raise their veils: they all have beards, some fair, some
black, some white and some grizzled. {i6}"The giant Malambruno has
enchanted us, and no one can disenchant us but the valiant Don Quixote,
who must go and fight him in the kingdom of Candaya, three thousand
leagues from here, give or take a few. Will the knight come?"{i7}

/MSG dolorida_acepta
{i4}"I will do, lady, all that I can, with the help of God."{i7}

In come four wild men dressed in ivy carrying a great wooden horse on
their shoulders. {i6}"This is Clavileño the Swift, who is steered by a
peg in his forehead. Knight and squire must mount, one in the saddle
and the other on the crupper. And both must go with their eyes
blindfolded, for otherwise they will grow giddy with the height."{i7}

/MSG montar_clavileno
You mount Clavileño. Sancho climbs onto the crupper, complaining that
it is not made of velvet, and blindfolds himself with a duenna's
handkerchief. {i5}"Sirs, commend us to God, for we're off to this
Candaya."{i7}

/MSG vuelo
You turn the peg. The duennas and everyone present raise their voices:
{i6}"God guide thee, valiant knight! Now, now you are going through the
air, cleaving it swifter than an arrow!"{i7}

With some great bellows they blow wind at you. {i4}"Without a doubt,
Sancho, we must be reaching the second region of the air, where the
hail and the snow are formed."{i7} Then they warm your faces with
lighted tow on the end of a cane. {i5}"Kill me if we aren't in the
region of fire already, for a good part of my beard has been
singed!"{i7}

At last, as Clavileño's belly is full of crackers, they set fire to him
and he flies through the air with a strange noise, and drops you both
on the ground, half singed. The duennas have vanished. In the garden
there is a lance stuck in the ground with a parchment: {i6}"The
illustrious knight Don Quixote of La Mancha ended and achieved the
adventure of the Countess Trifaldi, otherwise called the Distressed
Duenna, by merely attempting it."{i7}

/MSG consejos
The duke, delighted, keeps the promise he made: Sancho shall be
governor of an island. Before he leaves, you shut yourself in with him
and give him advice: {i4}"First of all, my son, thou must fear God, for
in the fear of Him is wisdom. Thou must keep in view what thou art,
striving to know thyself. Take pride in the humbleness of thy lineage.
Let the tears of the poor man find more compassion, but not more
justice, than the pleadings of the rich. Eat not garlic nor onions,
lest they find out thy boorish origin by the smell."{i7}

{i5}"If your worship thinks I'm not fit for this government, I give it
up here and now, for I'd sooner go to heaven as Sancho than to hell as
a governor."{i7}

{i4}"For those last words thou hast spoken, I judge thou deservest to
be governor of a thousand islands."{i7}

/MSG ahora_sancho
{i5}And from here the history leaves Don Quixote at the castle and
follows Sancho. Now you are Sancho Panza, governor.{i7}

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: BARATARIA (you are Sancho)
# =====================================================================
/MSG insula_llega
You are received with the ringing of bells and every sign of general
rejoicing. They lead you with great pomp to the great church to give
thanks to God, and then they hand you the keys of the town and accept
you as perpetual governor of the island of Barataria. Nobody quite
knows what you are smiling about.

/MSG pleito
Two old men come in. One carries a cane for a staff. The other, who
has none, speaks: {i6}"Sir, some days ago I lent this good man ten gold
crowns, and I've asked him for them many times, and not only does he
not give them back, but he denies it, and says I never lent him any ten
crowns, and that if I did, he has already returned them."{i7}

The one with the staff, in order to swear that he returned them, gives
the staff to the other to hold while he swears, as if it were in his
way. He swears on the cross of the wand. Then the lender gives him
back his staff, convinced. The old man with the staff bows and gets
ready to leave.

/MSG baculo_coger
{i5}"Give me that staff, good man, for I have need of it."{i7} The old
man gives it to you, with a face you can't read.

/MSG baculo_roto
You break the cane in half. Out of it fall ten crowns in gold. Everyone
is amazed and takes their governor for a new Solomon. {i5}"He who swears
he has returned what he holds in his hand swears a great deal and
returns very little."{i7}

The lender goes off with his money. The one who swore, with his shame.
The chronicler writes it all down.

/MSG medico_quita
As soon as you reach out for a dish, the man with the wand touches it,
and a page whisks it away as swiftly as if it were enchantment.
{i6}"That fruit is too moist, and that other dish is too hot and has
too many spices. And the partridges... {i2}omnis saturatio mala,
perdices autem pessima{i6}."{i7}

/MSG medico_huye
{i5}"Well then, Señor Doctor Pedro Recio of Bad Omen, native of
Tirteafuera, a place on the right-hand side as we go from Caracuel to
Almodóvar del Campo, get out of my sight at once. If not, I swear by
the sun I'll take a cudgel and, cudgelling away, beginning with him,
I'll not leave a doctor in the whole island."{i7}

The doctor slinks out of the room with his wand between his legs.

/MSG gobernador_come
They serve you a hash of beef with plenty of onion and some boiled
calves' feet, somewhat past their best. You eat with more relish than
if they had given you francolins of Milan, pheasants of Rome or geese
of Lavajos. When you have finished, a letter arrives from the duke:
{i6}"Certain enemies of mine and yours mean to make a furious assault
on that island, I know not on what night. It is advisable to keep
watch and be on the alert."{i7}

/MSG ronda_llega
In one street you come upon two men quarrelling over a gaming house.
You send one home and the other out of the island, with the chronicler
writing it all down. Further on, the constables bring in a girl dressed
as a man, as lovely as a thousand pearls, who won't say who she is.

/MSG doncella
The girl, weeping, confesses: {i6}"I am the daughter of Pedro Pérez
Mazorca, who farms the wool tax of this town. My father has kept me
shut up for ten years, and I only wanted to see the world, or at least
the town where I was born."{i7}

{i5}"This is a piece of childishness, and to tell such nonsense there
was no need for so much beating about the bush."{i7} You send her home
to her father, with her brother, who is also wandering about disguised
as a woman. The chronicler can't keep up.

/MSG invasion
At midnight you are woken by the bells and by voices: {i6}"To arms, to
arms, señor governor, to arms! Countless enemies have entered the
island, and we are lost unless your skill and valour come to our
aid!"{i7}

They fit you with two big shields, one in front and one behind, tied
with cords, so that you are boarded up and planked in, as straight as
a spindle, unable to bend your knees or move a single step. {i6}"Come
on, señor governor, lead us and encourage us all!"{i7} You take one
step and fall to the ground like a tortoise. All night long they run
over you, slashing at the shields, until they shout: {i6}"Victory,
victory! The enemy is in retreat!"{i7}

They untie you. You are battered, and very thoughtful.

/MSG renuncia
You dress in silence, go to the stable and embrace the dapple, and give
him a kiss of peace on the forehead. Then you saddle him, mount, and
turn to all those watching you:

{i5}"Make way, gentlemen, and let me go back to my old freedom. I was
not born to be a governor, or to defend islands or cities from the
enemies who want to attack them. Ploughing and digging I understand
better than giving laws. Naked I was born, naked I find myself: I
neither lose nor gain."{i7}

Your government has lasted ten days. They offer you company, and you
don't want it: you ask only for a little barley for the dapple and
half a cheese and half a loaf for yourself.

/MSG sima_cae
On the way to the castle night falls upon you and the dapple, and among
some ruins the two of you fall into a very deep pit. You break nothing,
which you take for a miracle, but down there nobody can hear you.

/MSG vuelve_dq
{i5}And here the history leaves Sancho in his pit and goes back to Don
Quixote, who is you once more.{i7}

One morning you ride out of the castle to practise what you would have
to do in the event of a combat, and Rocinante very nearly puts his feet
into a pit.

/MSG voces_sima
Great cries come out of the pit: {i5}"Hallo up there! Is there any
Christian who hears me, or any charitable knight who will take pity on
a sinner buried alive, on a wretched ungoverned governor?"{i7}

/MSG sacas_sancho
You let the rope down into the pit. Sancho ties the dapple, then
himself, and with the help of some of the duke's servants who come at
your shouts you bring the two of them out into the light of day.
Sancho, on his knees, gives thanks to God. The dapple brays.

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: BARCELONA AND THE KNIGHT OF THE WHITE MOON
# =====================================================================
/MSG barcelona_llega
You enter Barcelona on Midsummer Eve, at night, and in the morning you
see the sea for the first time. Don Antonio Moreno, a rich and discreet
gentleman, a friend of the bandit Roque Guinart, lodges you in his
house and walks you through the city with a placard on your back that
says: {i6}This is Don Quixote of La Mancha.{i7} You believe they
recognise you by your fame.

In one room of his house there is a head, apparently of bronze, on a
jasper table. Don Antonio says it answers whatever it is asked.

/MSG cabeza
You go up to the head and ask it in its ear: {i4}"Was it truth or
was it a dream, what I say happened to me in the cave of Montesinos?
Will Sancho's lashes come true? Will Dulcinea's disenchantment take
effect?"{i7}

{i6}"As for the cave, there is much to be said: it has something of
everything. Sancho's lashes will go slowly. Dulcinea's disenchantment
will be duly accomplished."{i7}

Sancho asks whether he will have another government. {i6}"Thou shalt
govern in thy house, and if thou return to it, thou shalt see thy wife
and children."{i7} {i5}"I could have told you that myself!"{i7} (Inside
the table there is a student, Don Antonio's nephew, who speaks through
a tin tube. Don't tell Sancho.)

/MSG blanca_luna
Along the beach a knight comes towards you, armed from head to foot,
with a shining moon painted on his shield.

{i6}"Illustrious knight and never sufficiently extolled Don Quixote of
La Mancha: I am the Knight of the White Moon. I come to do battle with
thee and prove the might of thy arm, to the end that I make thee
acknowledge and confess that my lady, whoever she may be, is
incomparably fairer than thy Dulcinea del Toboso. If thou fightest and
I vanquish thee, thou shalt lay down thine arms and retire to thy
village for the space of a year. And if thou vanquish me, my head shall
be at thy disposal, and the spoils of my arms and horse shall be
thine."{i7}

/MSG blanca_acepta
{i4}"Knight of the White Moon, whose achievements have never yet come to
my knowledge: I will make thee swear that thou hast never seen the
illustrious Dulcinea. I accept thy challenge, and at once, so that the
day thou hast fixed may not pass."{i7}

/MSG derrota
You both wheel your horses at the same moment. The Knight of the White
Moon, being swifter, meets you two-thirds of the way along the course,
and there he encounters you with such mighty force, without touching
you with his lance (for he raises it, it seems, on purpose), that he
brings Rocinante and you to the ground in a perilous fall.

He sets his lance against your visor: {i6}"Vanquished you are, knight,
and dead too, if you do not confess the conditions of our
challenge."{i7}

Bruised and stunned, without raising your visor, as if speaking from
inside a tomb, in a weak and feeble voice: {i4}"Dulcinea del Toboso is
the fairest woman in the world, and I the most unfortunate knight on
earth; it is not fitting that this truth should suffer by my
feebleness. Drive thy lance home, sir knight, and take my life, since
thou hast taken away my honour."{i7}

{i6}"That will I not, in sooth. Live the fame of the lady Dulcinea
del Toboso's beauty undimmed as ever. I am satisfied that the great
Don Quixote should retire to his own home for a year."{i7} And you
promise it, as a punctilious and true knight.

/MSG blanca_sanson
Don Antonio follows the victor to his inn and finds out: the Knight of
the White Moon is the bachelor Samson Carrasco, who, defeated in the
wood as the Knight of the Mirrors, swore to come back to send you home
and see whether that might cure you.

You leave Barcelona. As you pass the spot where you were overthrown,
you turn to look at it: {i4}"Here Troy was. Here my ill fortune, not
my cowardice, robbed me of all the glory I had won. Here my star fell
never to rise again."{i7} Your armour rides on the dapple, hung from a
tree like a trophy that moves.

# =====================================================================
#  MESSAGES: THE WAY HOME AND THE TWO ENDINGS
# =====================================================================
/MSG ante_dulcinea
All the characters you have met lead you before Dulcinea...

The curate and the barber, Samson Carrasco with his mirrors and his
moon, the innkeeper castellan, Maritornes, the Biscayan, the lion
keeper, Basilio and Quiteria, Montesinos, Master Pedro with his ape,
the Distressed Duenna without her beard, the dukes, and Doctor Recio,
who won't let anyone eat. Even Rocinante seems to know where he is
going.

/MSG dulcinea_1989
{i3}You got it right!! Dulcinea is the peasant girl who is
bewitched.{i7}

The jewel has broken the spell and the two of you live happily for
ever and ever. And as Don Quixote you will be remembered for ever.

{i5}(That is how the Aventuras Level 10 adventure ended in 1989.
Cervantes would not have approved, but then again nobody would have
finished it.){i7}

/MSG princesa_1989
You give the jewel to the one who looks like a princess. You have got
it wrong: Dulcinea is the other one, for she is bewitched. The two of
them go away and you have not managed to finish this adventure.

/MSG agueros
On the threshing floors of the village, two boys are quarrelling:
{i6}"Don't tire yourself, Periquillo, you'll never see it in all the
days of your life."{i7} And a hare, fleeing from some greyhounds, comes
to take shelter under the dapple's feet.

{i4}"Malum signum! Malum signum! Hare flies, greyhounds chase: Dulcinea
appears not!"{i7}

Sancho picks up the hare, gives it to the boys in exchange for a cage
of crickets they have, and puts it in your hands: {i5}"This hare is
Dulcinea, and I've caught her. What bad omen is that?"{i7}

/MSG calentura
They put you to bed. A fever comes on you that keeps you in bed for six
days, during which you are visited many times by the curate, the
bachelor and the barber, your friends, while Sancho Panza, your good
squire, never leaves your bedside. They try to cheer you up: the
bachelor has written an eclogue and wants you all to become shepherds,
Sancho as Pancino and you as the shepherd Quixotiz.

The doctor takes your pulse and says that, to be on the safe side, you
should attend to the health of your soul, for that of your body is in
danger.

/MSG cuerdo
You sleep at a stretch, as they say, for more than six hours. When you
wake you give a loud cry:

{i4}"Blessed be Almighty God, who has shown me such goodness! My reason
is now free and clear, rid of the dark shadows of ignorance that my
unhappy constant study of those detestable books of chivalry cast over
it. Now I see through their absurdities and deceptions. Congratulate
me, good sirs, that I am no longer Don Quixote of La Mancha, but Alonso
Quijano, whose way of life won for him the name of Good."{i7}

They send for a notary. You want to make your will.

/MSG sancho_llora
{i5}"Ah! Don't die, master, but take my advice and live many years, for
the greatest madness a man can commit in this life is to let himself
die without more ado. Don't be lazy, but get up from that bed, and let
us go out into the fields dressed as shepherds, as we agreed: perhaps
behind some bush we'll find the lady Dulcinea disenchanted, as fine as
fine can be."{i7}

/MSG testamento
{i4}"Item, it is my will that, touching certain moneys in the hands of
Sancho Panza, whom in my madness I made my squire, no account be
demanded of him. And if, as when I was mad I had a share in giving him
the government of the island, I could now, being in my senses, give
him that of a kingdom, it should be his.

Item, I leave all my estate to Antonia Quijana, my niece. And if she
should wish to marry, let her marry a man of whom it has first been
ascertained that he does not know what books of chivalry are."{i7}

/MSG muerte_alonso
Amid the tears and lamentations of those present, you give up the
ghost; that is to say, you die. The notary testifies that he has never
read in any book of chivalry of any knight errant having died in his
bed so calmly and so like a Christian as Don Quixote.

The bachelor places this epitaph upon your tomb:

{i6}He held the world as nothing;{n}its scarecrow and its
bogey,{n}and so it was that, in the end,{n}his fortune proved it best{n}
to live a madman, die a sage.{i7}

And the pen of Cide Hamete Benengeli, hung up by a brass wire on a
rack, says: {i5}"For me alone was Don Quixote born, and I for him; it
was his to act, mine to write."{i7}

{i6}{b1}THE END{b0}{i7}

/MSG muerte_tortuga
Without the armour of the shields or of common sense, you stay in the
street waiting for the enemy. The enemy, being make-believe, never
comes. What comes is the cold of dawn, and then shame. You leave the
island that same night, and the history, not knowing what to do with
you, ends here.

/MSG creditos2
{i6}DON QUIXOTE OF LA MANCHA{i7}{n}Part Two{n}(c) AVENTURAS LEVEL 10{n}{n}Part Two keeps the
puzzles of 1989: the Knight of the Mirrors, the lion who falls asleep
to a song and the two girls who both say they are Dulcinea.
